Why AGPL rather than a permissive licence
-----------------------------------------
CASCADE is offered as a hosted platform as well as source. Section 13 of the
AGPL ("Remote Network Interaction") is the operative clause: anyone who runs a
modified version as a network service must offer its source to the users of that
service. A permissive licence would allow a closed, hosted fork; this one does
not.

This constrains recipients, not the copyright holders. The authors retain
copyright and may therefore relicense future versions, grant commercial
exceptions, or extract components into a proprietary product, as ADR-0009
("engine public now, private-submodule deferred") anticipates. Note that this
freedom depends on the authors holding copyright in the whole work: a
contribution merged without a contributor licence agreement or a Developer
Certificate of Origin sign-off removes that option for the affected files.

Anything already released under this licence stays released under it. AGPL binds
only distribution and network-service provision — running, reading, modifying and
citing the code for research carries no obligation whatsoever.


Third-party components
----------------------
This repository's dependencies are free/open-source under permissive terms
(predominantly MIT, ISC, BSD-2/3-Clause and Apache-2.0); see
CASCADE-app/package-lock.json and CASCADE-backend/requirements*.txt for the
resolved sets. Notable non-MIT/BSD cases, none of which constrain this project's
outbound licence:

  * jszip                     dual-licensed (MIT OR GPL-3.0-or-later); used under MIT.
  * @img/sharp-*              LGPL-3.0-or-later native binaries, used unmodified
                              through a dynamic boundary.
  * certifi, and ~14 npm packages
                              MPL-2.0, whose copyleft is file-level and is not
                              triggered because the files are used unmodified.

The Inter typeface is redistributed in this repository as a binary font file
(CASCADE-app/app/fonts/Inter-Variable.woff2) under the SIL Open Font License 1.1,
whose text accompanies it in the same directory. The OFL governs the font
software alone and places no condition on the documents or programs that use it.

PostgreSQL, Caddy and Zitadel are deployed as separate container images. They are
aggregated with this work, not derived from it or linked into it.

The water-distribution benchmarks bundled under raw-networks/ (Net1, Net2, Net3,
Modena, C-Town) are third-party public research networks and remain under their
own terms. The three Italian aqueduct exports used in the paper are proprietary
utility data, are not distributed here, and are referred to throughout only as
Aqueduct A, B and C.
